## Fixed and verified this session

- **"2+2 split-brain" resolved**: it was wedged leftover state (stale module on test3/4), not code — run.sh's srcversion gate caught it; VM recycle cleared it. Clean runs converge 4/4 every time.
- **PR UA fence-out (proven root, fixed, verified)**: prep_fs's stale-PR CLEAR pends a "Reservations preempted" UA; the mount-time PROUT REGISTER consumed it, failed CHECK CONDITION (rc=2), and mxfs silently ran unregistered under a WE-RO reservation → whole suite 0/4 with EBADE writes. Fix: bounded UA retry in all five PAL PR wrappers + CAW mount now hard-fails rather than joining unfenced (v0.6.1, `pal/linux/kern.c`, `dlm/v5_mount.c`). cache_coherency went 0/4 → 4/4 immediately after.
- **Tombstone epoch continuity** (task from session 1) implemented and riding all runs since — not implicated in any failure.
- **Infra**: unique iSCSI IQNs set on test5–32 (cloned-image collision hazard); mpath_up.sh now normalizes IQNs at bring-up (test1 still pending its first re-login); dirland/dirwr instrumentation workflow established (`dirwr=1 dirland=1` modargs, ring dump via `dland_dump`).

## The remaining 4/caw blocker — phantom cached EX (task #7/#8, in progress)

Proven with completion-time tracing: a bast-release's unlock races a concurrent local re-acquire; the release clears the freshly re-granted slot bit, leaving in-core CACHED-EX with the disk released — two nodes then RMW the same dir block uncoordinated (~60 ms dual-writer window captured) and one node's dirents are durably lost. The P106 verify detects this but is explicitly probe-only; TCP fixed the identical race with a gen-aware release (-ESTALE → stranded → BAST re-arm) that the CAW dispatch ignored.

Validation so far: `dir_ex_revalidate=1` cut failures ~100%→~20%; my first two partial closures (entry-anchored seq abort on build 8D000BFD, wrapper-level gen pre-check on 6A7A8038) still failed — consistent with the identified anchor gap. **The tree now contains the complete fix** (un-built): `mxfs_dlm_caw_unlock_gen()` threads the release-decision anchor into the CAS loop itself, plus the `releasing` flag gating the no-CAS already-held grant path.

